---
import DocsLayout from "../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import PageBody from "../components/PageBody.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import Code from "../components/Code.astro";
import { defineSections } from "../lib/sections";
import { route } from "../lib/catalogue";
import starter from "../../public/starter.html?raw";

const features = [
  { icon: "palette", title: "Material Design 3 tokens", text: "Color, typography, shape and elevation tokens, with the whole color scheme generated from one source color." },
  { icon: "contrast", title: "Light and dark themes", text: "Set theme=\"light\" or theme=\"dark\" on the root element, or use auto to follow the operating system." },
  { icon: "code", title: "Semantic HTML", text: "The element is the component: an article is a card and a label wrapping a checkbox is a switch." },
  { icon: "accessibility_new", title: "Accessible components", text: "Interactive components manage focus, keyboard navigation and ARIA state for you." },
  { icon: "bolt", title: "No build step required", text: "Link one stylesheet and one script, or import only the components you use from the ES module and Sass entries." },
  { icon: "monitoring", title: "Charts included", text: "Line, column, bar, pie, radar and heatmap charts, each with an accessible data table." },
];

const demo = `<article class="outlined">
  <header>
    <h4>Trip settings</h4>
    <p class="subhead">Weekend in the mountains</p>
  </header>
  <p>Every color here comes from theme tokens, so the card follows light and dark mode.</p>
  <p>
    <input type="checkbox" class="chip-input" id="demo-hiking" checked>
    <label class="chip" for="demo-hiking">
      <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">check</span>
      Hiking
    </label>
    <input type="checkbox" class="chip-input" id="demo-camping">
    <label class="chip" for="demo-camping">
      <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">check</span>
      Camping
    </label>
  </p>
  <label class="switch">
    <input type="checkbox" checked>
    Download offline maps
  </label>
  <div class="actions">
    <button type="button" class="text">Cancel</button>
    <button type="button" class="filled">Save</button>
  </div>
</article>`;

const S = defineSections([
  { id: "features", label: "Features" },
  { id: "demo", label: "Code demo" },
  { id: "download", label: "Installation" },
  { id: "setup", label: "Setup" },
  { id: "templates", label: "Templates" },
  { id: "smaller-builds", label: "Smaller builds" },
  { id: "sass", label: "Sass", heading: "Sass setup" },
]);
---
<DocsLayout page="index">
<div slot="banner" class="docs-hero-actions">
  <p>
    <a class="button filled" href="#download">Get started</a>
    <a class="button tonal" href={route("buttons")}>Browse components</a>
    <a class="button text" href="https://github.com/BaezFJ/ExpressiveCSS">GitHub</a>
  </p>
  <p><code>npm install @expressivecss/expressive</code></p>
</div>
<PageBody sections={S}>
  <Section {...S.features}>
    <div class="docs-features">
      {features.map((f) => (
        <article class="filled">
          <span class="material-symbols primary-text" aria-hidden="true">{f.icon}</span>
          <h4>{f.title}</h4>
          <p>{f.text}</p>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section {...S.demo}>
    <p>This card, its filter chips and its switch are plain HTML. Switch the theme from the app bar to see the
      tokens change.</p>
    <div class="docs-demo" data-markdown-ignore set:html={demo} />
    <Code code={demo} />
  </Section>

  <Section {...S.download}>
                    <p class="flow-text">
                        Expressive comes in two forms. Pick the one that fits your
                        preference and experience.
                        The project is at version <code>0.15.0</code> and still growing, so the usual path is to build
                        from the repository.
                    </p>
                    <div class="row">
                        <div class="s12 m6">
                            <h5>Expressive</h5>
                            <p>
                                The standard version includes minified and unminified CSS and
                                JavaScript files.
                                Build the project, then copy the files from <code>dist/</code>. This option
                                needs little to no setup.
                                Use it if you are unfamiliar with Sass.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="s12 m6">
                            <h5>Sass</h5>
                            <p>
                                This version is the source SCSS in <code>src/sass</code>. It gives you more control over
                                which layers to include. It needs a Sass compiler.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <h5>From source</h5>
                    <p>Clone the repository and build the compiled CSS and JavaScript:</p>
                    <Code code={`
npm install
npm run build
`} />
                    <p>The compiled assets are written to <code>dist/</code>:</p>
                    <Code code={`
dist/
├── css/
│   ├── expressive.css
│   └── expressive.min.css
├── fonts/
├── js/
│   ├── expressive.cjs
│   ├── expressive.js
│   ├── expressive.min.js
│   ├── expressive.mjs
│   └── modular.mjs
└── types/
`} />

                    <h5>NPM</h5>
                    <p>
                        The package name is <code>@expressivecss/expressive</code>. It contains the source files and
                        the compiled CSS and JavaScript files.
                    </p>
                    <Code code={`
npm install @expressivecss/expressive
`} />

                    <h5>Yarn</h5>
                    <p>You can also add the package with yarn.</p>
                    <Code code={`
yarn add @expressivecss/expressive
`} />

                    <p>
                        Using a coding agent? <a href={route("ai")}>AI agents</a> covers the agent skills,
                        the MCP server, and the plain-text docs.
                    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section {...S.setup}>
                    <h5>Project structure</h5>
                    <p>After building, copy the compiled files into your website's directory. It will look
                        something like the tree below.</p>
                    <p>
                        There are two sets of files. The
                        <code>min</code> files are compressed to reduce load times. Use the minified files
                        in production and the unminified files during development.
                    </p>
                    <Code code={`
MyWebsite/
  |--css/
  |  |--expressive.css
  |  |--expressive.min.css
  |
  |--fonts/
  |  |--material-symbols-outlined.woff2
  |
  |--js/
  |  |--expressive.js
  |  |--expressive.min.js
  |
  |--index.html
`} />

                    <h5>HTML setup</h5>
                    <p>
                        Next, link the files in your webpage, as in the example below. Load JavaScript files at
                        the end of the body to reduce page load time.
                    </p>
                    <Code code={`
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en" theme="light">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="css/expressive.min.css">
  </head>
  <body>
    <script src="js/expressive.min.js"></script>
    <script>
      document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
        Expressive.AutoInit();
      });
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
`} />

                    <h5>Initialize JavaScript</h5>
                    <p>
                        Call <code>AutoInit()</code> after the page has loaded so components such as navigation
                        rails, tooltips, and tabs start themselves. Importing the JavaScript does not call it for
                        you. The import does install shared document behaviors (forms, chips, cards, and a few
                        others). The browser bundle exposes the framework as the global <code>Expressive</code>
                        object.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        <code>AutoInit()</code> scans <code>document.body</code> by default. Pass a container to limit
                        the scan, or add the
                        <code>no-autoinit</code> class to an element you want to initialize yourself.
                    </p>
                    <Code code={`
Expressive.AutoInit();

Expressive.AutoInit(document.querySelector('#app'), {
  Tooltip: { position: 'top' }
});
`} />

                    <h5>ES modules</h5>
                    <p>Import the framework or individual components from the module build:</p>
                    <Code code={`
import { AutoInit, Tooltip } from './js/expressive.mjs';

AutoInit();

const element = document.querySelector('.custom-tooltip');
Tooltip.init(element, { position: 'top' });
`} />

                    <h5>Themes</h5>
                    <p>
                        Set the <code>theme</code> attribute on the root element.
                        <code>light</code> and <code>dark</code> lock the scheme.
                        <code>auto</code>, or no attribute at all, follows the OS.
                    </p>
                    <Code code={`
<html lang="en" theme="auto">
`} />
                    <Code code={`
document.documentElement.setAttribute('theme', 'dark');
`} />
  </Section>

  <Section {...S.templates}>
                    <p><a class="button tonal" href={route("layouts")}>Browse layout templates</a></p>
                    <h5>Basic starter</h5>
                    <p>Download this HTML file as <code>index.html</code> and put the framework's
                        <code>dist/</code> directory beside it. It includes an app bar, responsive cards,
                        a tooltip, and a theme that follows your system settings. You do not need a bundler.</p>
                    <p><a class="button filled" href="/starter.html" download="index.html">Download starter HTML</a>
                        <a class="button outlined" href="/starter.html">Preview starter</a></p>
                    <p>From a built repository checkout, create a separate starter directory:</p>
                    <Code code={`
mkdir my-site
cp docs/public/starter.html my-site/index.html
cp -R dist my-site/dist
`} />
                    <p>If you installed the npm package, copy its built assets instead:</p>
                    <Code code={`
cp -R node_modules/@expressivecss/expressive/dist my-site/dist
`} />
                    <p>Keep the <code>css/</code>, <code>js/</code>, and <code>fonts/</code> directories together.</p>
                    <Code code={`
my-site/
├── index.html
└── dist/
    ├── css/
    ├── js/
    └── fonts/
`} />
                    <p>Open <code>my-site/index.html</code> in your browser. Edit the title and content to start your project,
                        or copy the complete template below.</p>
                    <Code code={starter} />
                    <h5>Layout reference</h5>
                    <p class="flow-text">
                        These two pages show how Expressive
                        structures content.
                        You can copy the markup they use.
                    </p>
                    <div class="row">
                        <div class="s12 m6">
                            <article class="center-align">
                                <span class="material-symbols medium primary-text" aria-hidden="true">grid_on</span>
                                <h5>Grid</h5>
                                <p class="left-align">
                                    The 12-column CSS Grid, containers, offsets, and responsive layouts. Start here
                                    after you link the stylesheet.
                                </p>
                                <a class="button filled" href={route("grid")}>Open Grid</a>
                            </article>
                        </div>
                        <div class="s12 m6">
                            <article class="center-align">
                                <span class="material-symbols medium primary-text" aria-hidden="true">tune</span>
                                <h5>Helpers</h5>
                                <p class="left-align">
                                    Alignment, visibility, spacing, truncation, and other single-purpose classes you
                                    will use on every page.
                                </p>
                                <a class="button outlined" href={route("helpers")}>Open Helpers</a>
                            </article>
                        </div>
                    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section {...S["smaller-builds"]}>
    <p>Use the modular entry with a bundler to include selected JavaScript components.
      Initialize each component after its markup exists, and destroy instances when you remove the view.</p>
    <Code code={`import { Tabs } from '@expressivecss/expressive/modular';
const tabs = Tabs.init(document.querySelector('.tabs'));`} />
    <p>This entry installs no document behaviors. Call each behavior your page needs once,
      such as <code>Forms.Init()</code> or <code>Dialogs.Init()</code>.
      The root import keeps its existing initialization. <code>AutoInit</code>
      is still available and includes the full component registry.</p>
    <p>Select Sass partials with the custom entry. With Sass's Node package importer,
      compile with <code>sass --pkg-importer=node app.scss app.css</code>.</p>
    <Code code={`@use "pkg:@expressivecss/expressive/scss/custom" with (
  $components: ("icons-material-design", "tabs"),
  $utilities: ()
);`} />
    <p>Tokens and base styles stay included. Lists load in the order written.
      <code>null</code> includes the full group, and <code>()</code> omits it.
      Include supporting partials for the features you use. The default configuration
      produces the same CSS as the full entry.</p>
    <p>For FormSelect and Datepicker, select <code>icons-material-design</code>,
      <code>buttons</code>, <code>menu</code>, <code>forms</code>, and
      <code>datepicker</code>, in that order. Initialize both components explicitly.</p>
    <p>To supply smaller icon fonts, set <code>$expressive-include-fonts: false</code>
      and provide your own font faces, including text fonts. Your subset must include icons
      from dynamic content and generated controls. Keep full fonts if icon names are unrestricted.</p>
    <p>The <a href="https://github.com/BaezFJ/ExpressiveCSS#reduce-page-downloads">README</a>
      contains the initialization table, complete Sass recipes, and font declarations.</p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.sass}>
                    <p class="flow-text">You only need this section if you chose the Sass sources.</p>
                    <h5>Compiling Sass</h5>
                    <p>
                        Besides compiled CSS, the repository contains many <code>.scss</code> files with the
                        styles of individual layers
                        and components. Browsers cannot read Sass, so compile <code>src/sass/expressive.scss</code>
                        into a regular CSS file.
                        Then link the compiled file in your HTML page.
                    </p>
                    <p>From the repository, the npm script does that for you:</p>
                    <Code code={`
npm run build:css
`} />
                    <p>In another Sass project, use the framework's Sass entry point:</p>
                    <Code code={`
@use "@expressivecss/expressive/src/sass/expressive";
`} />
                    <p>Inside this repository, the entry point is
                        <code>src/sass/expressive.scss</code>.</p>
                    <Code code={`
MyWebsite/
|--css/
|  |--expressive.css <-- compiled from scss/expressive.scss
|
|--js/
|  |--expressive.js
|
|--scss/
|  |--expressive.scss
|  |--abstracts/
|  |--tokens/
|  |--utilities/
|  |--base/
|  |--components/
|
|--index.html
`} />
  </Section>
</PageBody>
</DocsLayout>
